Hi Team,

We are planning to migrate our Spring Boot 3.5.x applications and internal shared libraries from JDK 17 to JDK 21.

To minimize compatibility risks and ensure a smooth transition, we propose a phased migration approach as outlined below.

Phase 1: Docker Runtime Upgrade (JDK 17 → 21)

* Upgrade Docker base images to JDK 21 across all applications.
* Continue using existing applications and internal libraries compiled with JDK 17.
* Perform regression and compatibility testing before production rollout.

Phase 2: Application Compilation Upgrade (JDK 17 → 21)

* Upgrade application build pipelines and Maven configurations to JDK 21.
* Recompile applications targeting Java 21 while continuing to use existing Java 17-compiled internal libraries.
* Validate and deploy applications incrementally.

Phase 3: Internal Library Upgrade (JDK 17 → 21)

* Upgrade internal shared libraries to compile with JDK 21.
* Publish updated library versions to our Maven repository.
* Validate compatibility with all consuming applications.

Key Considerations:

* Java 17-compiled code is generally compatible with the Java 21 runtime.
* Each phase will include appropriate testing and validation before proceeding to the next.
* Changes will be rolled out incrementally to minimize risk and allow rollback if required.
* Existing Spring Boot 3.5.x versions will be retained during this migration.

This approach allows us to upgrade the runtime first, followed by application compilation and finally shared libraries, without requiring all components to be upgraded simultaneously.

Please review the proposed approach and share your feedback or concerns.

